--- machine\software\gxwi
canonical: Machine\Software\GXWI

Listener, compositor, machine overlay, and public sign-in settings.

gxwid reads this subtree. gxwi-server, the half that faces the network, runs
under a restricted token. gxwid reads the required configuration and passes
it to the worker at startup.

Every value is optional and has a compiled default. A value of the wrong
type, or one that makes no sense, is ignored and never mended: gxwid logs the
value it refused and what it is using instead.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and Administrators
full, Authenticated Users read). That descriptor is the only gate on
`Compositor`, and whoever can write `Compositor` chooses what runs as
everyone who logs on. It is the only gate on `OverlaySession` and
`OverlayUsername` too, and whoever can write those chooses what everyone who
reaches the machine is shown, and whose authority they are given without
logging on. Nothing in gxwid's environment can say any of this instead.

--- machine\software\gxwi listen
canonical: Machine\Software\GXWI Listen
type: REG_SZ
default: 127.0.0.1:7780
valid: an IP address and a port, as `0.0.0.0:443` or `[::]:443`; not a host name
applies: restart

The address and port GXWI serves on.

gxwid binds it once, as it starts, and keeps it across restarts of
gxwi-server, so connections made while the server is down wait rather than
being refused.

The default reaches nobody off the machine. The network policy also drops
inbound flows to a port no rule names, so serving other machines takes a rule
under `Machine\System\Network\Rules\Flow` as well as this.

--- machine\software\gxwi compositor
canonical: Machine\Software\GXWI Compositor
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent: `gxwi-hello`, the stand-in beside gxwid)
valid: an absolute path
applies: live (for new sessions)

The program a session runs.

gxwid starts it as a job carrying the token of whoever signed in, and hands
it the session's socket. It is read as each session starts: a change needs no
restart, and reaches nobody who is already signed in until they sign out.

This chooses what runs as everyone who logs on. Treat write access to it as
you would write access to the program itself.

A path that is not there is not refused here. The session fails to start,
and the person who signed in is told so.

--- machine\software\gxwi overlaysession
canonical: Machine\Software\GXWI OverlaySession
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent: no overlay)
valid: an absolute path
applies: live

A program that everyone who reaches GXWI is sent to, in place of the logon
pages and of any session: the overlay.

It is for a machine that is not, something to sign in to: one
running an installer, one still being set up, one that is starting.

While it is set, nobody is asked to sign in and nobody reaches a session,
their own included. Every request goes to the overlay, except for the few
paths under `/.gxwi/` that are GXWI's own. Sessions that were running go on
running behind it, and are there again when the value is removed.

There is one overlay for the machine, and everyone who reaches it shares it.
gxwid starts it as a job running as `OverlayUsername` and hands it a socket
as it hands a compositor its session's: the program finds a listening socket
as its first descriptor, puts its own name to it, and answers HTTP/2 there.
It is not that account's session, and the two are never taken for each
other: the overlay has a socket and a runtime directory of its own.

gxwid watches this key. Setting the value starts the overlay. Changing it,
or `OverlayUsername`, ends the one running and starts another. Removing it
ends the overlay and gives the logon pages back. None of these needs a
restart. An overlay that exits is started again.

An overlay that is set and cannot run is not replaced by the logon pages.
Whoever set it meant people to be kept from what is behind it, so they are
shown a page saying the machine is not ready yet, which asks again by itself.
gxwid keeps trying, 1 second later at first and then less often, up to every
30 seconds, and its log says why. This covers a program that is not
there, an `OverlayUsername` that is missing, and an account the authority
will not sign in without a credential.

Only a value that makes no sense in itself is ignored as other settings are,
and then there is no overlay.

--- machine\software\gxwi overlayusername
canonical: Machine\Software\GXWI OverlayUsername
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent: an overlay cannot run)
valid: an account's name, as it would be given at a logon page; at most 1024 bytes, no control characters
applies: live

The account the overlay runs as.

gxwid authenticates this account through the authority, using the same
logon type as a browser user (Remote Interactive), and tells the
authority that no credential can be collected. The authority therefore
grants the logon only for an account that needs no credential. An account
with a password or a key is refused and the overlay does not start, so an
overlay is never a way in to an account that has one.

Everyone who reaches the machine uses the overlay, and so acts with this
account's authority, without having said who they are. Name the account
with the least authority the program needs.

It does nothing without `OverlaySession`. With `OverlaySession` set and this
absent, or not text, or not a name as described above, the overlay cannot
run: there is no account an overlay runs as by default.

--- machine\software\gxwi\logon
canonical: Machine\Software\GXWI\Logon

Public machine labels and notices on the sign-in pages.

Both values are shown to anyone who can reach the port, before they have
said who they are. Put nothing here that is not for everyone to read.

--- machine\software\gxwi\logon name
canonical: Machine\Software\GXWI\Logon Name
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent: nothing is shown)
valid: 1 to 64 characters, none of them control characters
applies: restart

Public machine label shown under the heading of each sign-in page.

For someone with more than one machine to sign in to, deciding whether this
is the one they meant. It is a label and not the host name: nothing resolves
it.

--- machine\software\gxwi\logon notice
canonical: Machine\Software\GXWI\Logon Notice
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent: nothing is shown)
valid: at most 8 entries and 2000 characters in all, none of them control characters
applies: restart

A notice shown on the first logon page, above where the username is asked
for, a paragraph to each entry.

For the words an organization needs read before a logon: who can use the
machine, and that use is monitored. It is text and is shown as text. Markup
in it is shown as written.
